   257	\paragraph{Stronger attacks and where R3 fails (Q3).} Table~\ref{tab:q3-envelope} gives each core rule's attack envelope: the family with the highest training gain, evaluated unchanged. These are finite-search results, not bounds on the families. Against B3 the selected envelope reaches \site{r_q3_f000_f1} on fresh1; against R3 it is \site{r_q3_f111_f1} there, and against C$^*$ \site{r_q3_cs_f1}, from \site{r_q3_cs_f1_fam}. H4 was written in a separate session against the frozen defenders and committed by its hash before the lock; its author's containment note states that it contains D2+H3 exactly as a special case, so it extends the public family rather than offering a disjoint attack concept. Evaluated at the headline market only, its selected search gains \site{r_q3_f111_h4} against R3 and \site{r_q3_f000_h4} against B3. Robustness against D2+H3 is unanswered for every core rule because of search flags, and so is R3's robustness against X7-SC; X5-AG/v2 and H4 carry no search-quality claim. Welfare attacks are the clearest weakness. At the headline market, the selected W0 attacks (no profit constraint) yield gross efficiency \site{r_q3_w0_f000} for B3 and \site{r_q3_w0} for R3, with gains relative to the truthful reference of \site{r_q3_w0g_f000} and \site{r_q3_w0g_f111}; C$^*$ is like R3. Under the prescribed W1 searches (mean gain at least zero), the selected feasible attacks yield efficiency \site{r_q3_w1} for R3, while B3 stays at \site{r_q3_w1_f000} (Table~\ref{tab:q3-welfare}). These finite-search results are not lower bounds on efficiency over a broader policy class. An episode-level mixture, which picks a welfare attack or a profitable attack at the start of each episode, satisfies a mean-gain constraint only when its probability-weighted component gains satisfy that constraint; we examine such mixtures on the existing evaluation records. On the records used to select them, W0 weights of \site{r_ph_w_r3} for R3 and \site{r_ph_w_cs} for C$^*$, with t